  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe develop a multi-resolution binary optimization algorithm that combines grid-based adaptation and quantum-inspired mutation to escape local optima. Unlike continuous refinement, we use a coarse-to-fine binary evolution scheme that explicitly preserves the binary constraint throughout resolution changes. The core innovation is:\n1. **Population dynamics**: Maintains diverse sub-populations at different grid resolutions\n2. **Resolution transfer**: Upsampling preserves binary structure through interpolation while controlling the correlation spectrum\n3. **Quantum mutation**: Introduces probabilistic bit flips governed by a multi-directional potential function\n\nThe algorithm starts with a population of balanced binary vectors at 64 points. Generations alternate between:\n- Coarse grid exploration using crossover\n- Fine grid exploitation via simulated annealing\n- Adaptive resolution jumps controlled by fitness landscape analysis\n\nThis approach ensures both global exploration and local refinement without prematurely converging to continuous relaxations.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe minimize:\nC\u2085 = max_k (dx * \u03a3 h[i] * (1-h[i+k]) )   for k \u2208 [-(L-1), L-1]\n\nSubject to:\nh \u2208 {0,1}\u207f  \n\u03a3 h[i] = n/2  \n\nOur genetic representation is a binary sequence of length n. The fitness function is this maximum correlation estimate.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Validate input grid with n=64 (or specified value)\n2. Initialize population with:\n   - Thue-Morse sequence (if parent available, binarize)\n   - Random balanced vectors (ensure \u03a3 h[i] = n/2)\n   - Fractional phase shifts of known sequences\n3. For each generation:\n   a. Compute fitness via full correlation\n   b. Select parents via tournament selection\n   c. Crossover with:\n        - Variable-point crossover points\n        - Uniform crossover probability\n   d. Mutate with quantum-inspired mutation:\n        p_flip = min(0.1, sqrt(|\u0394F|)) where \u0394F is fitness difference\n4. After sufficient fitness progress:\n   a. Upsample selected vectors by linear interpolation\n   b. Rebinarize and ensure \u03a3 h[i] = n'/2 via local repair\n   c. Evaluate on new grid\n5. Simulated annealing on finest grid:\n   - Hill climbing with temperature decay\n   - Neighbors defined by multiple random bit flips\n\nSearch choices:\n- Population size: 50 vectors\n- Crossover probability: 0.8\n- Mutation rate: 0.05 (quantum-inspired)\n- Temp schedule: T0=0.01, \u03b1=0.95\n\nNumerical robustness:\n- Repair mechanism: When \u03a3 h[i] \u2260 n'/2, apply \u03b4 = 1 mutation until satisfied\n- Fall back: Seek alternate grid layouts when convergence stalls\n- Performance: ~800s per resolution level with adaptive cooling\n\nRuntime safeguards:\n1. Track fitness history; if stagnant, inject diversity mutations\n2. Time-allocate resolution increases based on compute budget\n3. Graceful degradation from continuous to binary counterparts\n\nReturn contract:\n- h_values: Final 256-point binary vector\n- c5_bound: Computed as (2.0/n)*max(np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full'))\n- n_points: 256\n\nThe search procedure combines several features:\n1. Multi-grid resolution evolution\n2. Probabilistic quantum mutation guided by fitness gradients\n3. Adaptive cooling schedule conditioning on grid size\n4. Explicit binary constraint preservation during interpolation\n\nFitness gradient estimates are computed by finite differences. For vectors v, the gradient perturbs one random bit to estimate dFitness/dr. If (n,i+1)*\u03b7 < current_fitness, stay; else accept. \u03b7 is fitness tolerance.",
